<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="photorespiration I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-181" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: EC-4.1.1.39|, commonly referred to as ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase or RubisCO, is a bifunctional enzyme that catalyzes both the carboxylation and oxygenation of |FRAME: D-RIBULOSE-15-P2| (RuBP). The carboxylation process is generally known as CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt; fixation (see |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY|) whereas the oxygenation process is known as photorespiration (this pathway). The two substrates (CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) are competitive with regard to RubisCO, so that an increase in CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; concentration inhibits oxygenation and vice versa |CITS:[Bowyer97]|. An important factor influencing the rate of oxygenation is temperature, as photorespiration is enhanced by high temperature. The reasons for this are twofold: (i) as temperatures increase the solubility of CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; diminishes more rapidly than that of O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and (ii) the specificity factor (&amp;Omega;) of RubisCO (which represents a measure of the relative specificity for CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) decreases with increasing temperatures between 7&amp;deg;C and 35&amp;deg;C. While the carboxylation reaction results in two molecules of |FRAME:G3P|, which are processed in the |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY|, the oxygenation reaction forms one molecule of |FRAME:G3P| and one molecule of |FRAME:CPD-67| (2PG). The latter cannot be used in the |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY| and, instead, is salvaged in the photorespiration pathway. While photorespiration is essential for the viability of all organisms that perform oxygenic photosynthesis, including cyanobacteria and C3 plants, at ambient CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; conditions |CITS: [18957552][26994478]|, the photorespiratory cycle results in carbon, nitrogen and energy losses, and, unlike true respiration, does not produce ATP or reducing power. As a matter of fact, despite its name photorespiration does not involve neither light nor respiration. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The main features of the photorespiration pathway include: (i) the conversion of |FRAME: CPD-67| into |FRAME: GLY|; (ii) the processing of two glycines into |FRAME: SER|, CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and ammonia; and (iii) the conversion of |FRAME: SER| into |FRAME: G3P|, which is directed towards CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt; fixation via the |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY|. The first step of the pathway involves the dephosphorylation of |FRAME:CPD-67|, which not only recycles phosphate but also prevents accumulation of this compound, which is a potent inhibitor of |FRAME: EC-5.3.1.1|. The next step is the oxidation of |FRAME:GLYCOLLATE| to |FRAME:GLYOX|. In plants |FRAME:GLYCOLLATE| is exported to the cytoplasm where it is oxidized in the peroxisomes to |FRAME:GLYOX| by |FRAME: EC-1.1.3.15|. The |FRAME:HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| generated during this step is detoxified by catalases (see |FRAME:DETOX1-PWY|; |CITS: [15012254]|) in the peroxisome. This step is catalyzed by a different enzyme in chlorophytes and cyanobacteria (see |FRAME: PWY-8363| and |FRAME: PWY-8363|). Glyoxylate is converted into |FRAME:GLY| by two different enzymes: |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.45| and |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.4|. One of the glycine molecues is converted by |FRAME: EC-1.4.1.27|, to CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;, ammonia, and a single carbon that is transferred to tetrahydrofolate, forming |FRAME: METHYLENE-THF-GLU-N 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate|. The methylene group is then transferred to the second glycine molecule by |FRAME: EC-2.1.2.1|, forming |FRAME: SER|. The serine molecue feeds back into |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.45|, closing a cycle. The main carbon molecule produced by this cycle is |FRAME:OH-PYR|, which is reduced into |FRAME:GLYCERATE| by |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.29|. The pathway ends with the phosphorylation of glycerate to |FRAME:G3P|, which re-enters the Calvin cycle. The phosphorylation is performed by different enzymes in cyanobacteria vs. other photosynthetic organisms. The outcome of photorespiration in photosynthetic cells is loss of carbon via CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; release, as well as loss of energy, yet the pathway is essential at atmospheric concentrations of CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;. The biological function of photorespiration is not clear. One possibility is that photorespiration is necessary under conditions of high light intensity and low CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; concentration to dissipate excess ATP and NADPH from the photosynthesis light reactions, and prevent damage to the photosynthetic apparatus |CITS: [11299007]|. Another explanation is that the pathway is necessary for the assimilation of nitrate |CITS: [15272076]|. Engineering tobacco plants by intorducing into the chloroplasts synthetic glycolate metabolic pathways that are thought to be more efficient than the native pathway and blocking glycolate export from the chloroplast resulted in improved photosynthetic quantum yield by 20-40% |CITS: [30606819]|.</Description>
