        <Point elementId="GLYCERATE-DEHYDROGENASE-RXN_end" arrowHead="Conversion" x="305.0" y="1000" elementRef="GLYCERATE" relX="-1.0" relY="0.0" />
        <Anchor elementId="GLYCERATE-DEHYDROGENASE-RXN_anchor" position="0.5" shapeType="Circle" />
      </Waypoints>
      <Graphics lineColor="000000" lineStyle="Solid" lineWidth="1.0" connectorType="Straight" />
      <Comment source="BioCyc">There is a certain confusion about the nomenclature of this enzyme. There are two redundant EC numbers describing the reaction - 1.1.1.29 and 1.1.1.81 |CITS: [8120891]|. The former refers to the enzyme as glycerate dehydrogenase, while the second refers to it as hydroxypyruvate reductase. The only difference is that entry 1.1.1.81 accomodates both NADH and NADPH as electron acceptors, while 1.1.1.29 accpets only NADH. Thus 1.1.1.29 is a specific case of 1.1.1.81. As a result, some of the literature refers to enzymes that accept NADH only as 1.1.1.29, while others use 1.1.1.81. We have chosen to use 1.1.1.81 in the serine cycle pathway, as the enzymes from some species (such as &lt;i&gt;M. extorquens&lt;/i&gt;) can utilize both NADH and NADPH.</Comment>
